# arcen: auth account
#
# The PAM policy the macOS Pier authenticates connecting clients against.
# Installed as /etc/pam.d/arcen, owned by root, mode 0644.
#
# This is deliberately Arcen's own service rather than a borrowed one. Reusing
# `login` or `sshd` would inherit their session modules, so a password check
# would start a launchd session and mount a home directory as a side effect.
# Arcen wants the question answered, not the consequences.
#
# `pam_opendirectory` is what actually answers it, so local accounts, directory
# accounts, disabled accounts and password policy all behave exactly as they do
# for any other macOS login, without Arcen reimplementing any of it.
#
# If this file is missing, PAM falls back to `other`, which on macOS is
# `pam_deny`. A host with no policy installed refuses everyone rather than
# admitting everyone, which is the correct direction to fail.
auth       required       pam_opendirectory.so
# `pam_nologin` honours /etc/nologin, so an administrator closing the machine
# for maintenance closes it to Arcen too.
account    required       pam_nologin.so
account    required       pam_opendirectory.so
#
# Deliberately no `pam_sacl` line.
#
# Two versions of this file tried one and both were wrong on a real machine.
# `sacl_service=ssh` required every Arcen user to also hold Remote Login
# rights, so a directory account entitled to use the Mac was refused. Changing
# it to `screensharing` did not help either: when no Service Access Control
# List group exists — which is the default on a freshly bound machine —
# `pam_sacl` *denies* rather than admitting everyone. A domain account with
# valid credentials was told "this account may not sign in here", which reads
# like a password problem and is not one.
#
# Access control therefore rests on the directory itself: `pam_opendirectory`
# applies the account's real status, expiry and password policy, and
# `pam_nologin` lets an administrator close the machine. An operator who wants
# to narrow Arcen to a named group can create the SACL group and add:
#
#     account    required       pam_sacl.so sacl_service=screensharing
#
# That is an explicit decision with a group behind it, rather than a default
# that locks out the people it was meant to admit.
